    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d191">

                <pb facs="0512" n="426" xml:id="pg426"/>

                <head>No. 189.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Sir Edward Thornton to Mr.
                    Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1873-06-19"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 19, 1873</date>.
                        (Received June 19.)</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer you to a note
                    addressed to Mr. Bancroft Davis by Mr. Pakenham on the 10th of September, 1871,
                    asking the consideration by the Government of the United States of a proposal
                    made by the government of Newfoundland that American fishermen should be
                    admitted to the right of taking seals within the territorial jurisdiction of
                    Newfoundland on the condition of the admission of the produce of the
                    Newfoundland seal-fishery into the United States free of duty.</p>
                <p>Mr. Bancroft Davis replied, on the 23d of the same month, that the subject would
                    receive consideration, but that the proposal was one which needed congressional
                    approval before it could be definitively accepted by the Department of
                    State.</p>
                <p>In compliance with an instruction which I have received this morning from Earl
                    Granville, I have the honor to inquire whether any steps have been taken by your
                    Department with a view to carry out the proposal made by the government of
                    Newfoundland, with regard to which Mr. Bancroft Davis was at the time informed
                    that Her Majesty’s government would be glad if it could be acceded to.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>EDW’D THORNTON.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
